32-value-data-model: a Curry value as a Python object.

Seam: equality, hash, order, truth, len and indexing of a node; the forward
node after an evaluation; the trusted annotation of curry.typed.
Sources: memo A9; catalogue P15, P16, P17, P18, P22.  Backends: cxx.
Oracle: none, the goals are Python.

Expected output: two values 42 are equal and hash apart, so a set keeps
both, and a node never equals the int 42 (P15).  sorted(coins) is a
TypeError, the nodes define no order; key=str works.  The node False is
true in Python: the C++ node has no __bool__.  A list value has no len;
n[0] is the head <Int 1> and n[1] the tail <: <Int 2> <[]>> (P16).  The
goal node inc 1 is <_Fwd <Int 2>> after its evaluation; a second
evaluation gives 2 through the recorded type, and curry.topython(e) returns
the forward node unchanged (P17).  curry.typed(e, 'Bool') types as Bool,
unchecked (P22).  The Python backend differs on three lines and the driver
is declared on the C++ backend alone: bool(f) is False, len(n) is 2 (the
successor count) and e after the evaluation is <Int 2>, which topython
converts to 2.  Pins no defect.
